{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-4-175-fall-2005", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["4.175"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Case Studies in City Form", "course_slug": "4-175-case-studies-in-city-form-fall-2005", "term": "Fall", "year": 2005, "as_taught_in": "Fall 2005", "language": "en", "departments": ["Architecture"], "academic_levels": ["Graduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Michael Dennis", "Mr. Anubhav Gupta"], "subject_primary": "Architecture", "subject_secondary": ["Architectural Design", "Social Science", "Urban Studies"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Fine Arts", "subtopics": [{"name": "Architecture", "specialties": ["Architectural Design"]}]}, {"name": "Social Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Urban Studies", "specialties": []}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": [], "description": "This course serves as an introduction to urban form and design, focusing on the physical, historical, and social form of cities. Selected cities are analyzed, drawn, and compared, to develop a working understanding of urban and architectural form. The development of map making and urban representation is discussed, and use of the computer is required. A special focus is placed on the historical development of the selected cities, especially mid-nineteenth and mid-twentieth century periods of expansion. Readings focus on urban design theory in the twentieth century and will be discussed during a weekly seminar on them. This is a methods class for S.M.Arch.S. students in Architecture and Urbanism.", "course_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/4-175-case-studies-in-city-form-fall-2005/", "image_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/4-175-case-studies-in-city-form-fall-2005/409639b3a577655476e946e68a6f20b0_4-175f05.jpg", "license_name": "CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/", "re